---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import DateTools from '../components/DateTools.tsx';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import { webApplicationLd, proseClass } from './_seo';
const title = 'Age, Date Difference and Timestamp Calculator | SizeTune';
const description =
  'Work out an exact age in years, months and days, count the days between two dates, or convert a Unix timestamp to a date in any time zone. Runs in your browser.';
const jsonLd = webApplicationLd('SizeTune: age, date difference and timestamp calculator', '/date-calculators/', description);
---
<Base pageType="tool" width="wide" title={title} description={description} path="/date-calculators/" jsonLd={jsonLd}>
  <Hero badge="Free · Calculated on this device · No sign-up" title="Age, dates and timestamps" lead="Three small calculators on one page: an exact age, the number of days between two dates, and Unix timestamps with a time zone. Nothing you type leaves your browser." />

  <DateTools client:load />
  <noscript>
    <p class="mt-4 rounded-xl border border-amber-400 bg-amber-50 p-4">
      These calculators run inside your browser, so they will not work while JavaScript is turned off. Enable JavaScript
      and reload to use them. The notes below explain how each answer is worked out.
    </p>
  </noscript>

  <div class={`mx-auto max-w-3xl mt-10 ${proseClass}`}>
    <h2>How the age is counted</h2>
    <p>
      The age calculator counts complete years first, then complete months, then the days left over. It starts from the
      date of birth and moves forward one month at a time. If a month is shorter than the birth day, the count lands on
      the last day of that month, so someone born on 31 January reaches one month on 28 February in an ordinary year, and
      on 29 February in a leap year.
    </p>
    <p>
      A person born on 29 February reaches each birthday on 28 February in years that have no leap day. If a form asks
      for age "as on" a closing date, type that date in the second box instead of leaving it empty. Leaving it empty uses
      today's date from your device.
    </p>

    <h2>Days between two dates</h2>
    <p>
      The result is the number of midnights between the two calendar days. The first day is not counted and the last day
      is, so from 1 March to 2 March is one day. Leap days are included, which is why 1 January to 1 March is 60 days in
      2024 and 59 in 2023. The answer is the same whichever date you enter first, and the note beside it tells you which
      date is later. Dates are compared as calendar days, so a daylight-saving change in your time zone cannot add or
      remove a day.
    </p>

    <h2>Unix timestamps and time zones</h2>
    <p>
      A Unix timestamp is the number of seconds since midnight UTC on 1 January 1970. It names a single moment, and the
      date you see depends on the time zone you read it in. The same timestamp can fall on different calendar days in
      Auckland and in Los Angeles, so the converter shows the zone next to the result.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Seconds or milliseconds.</strong> A number with 13 digits, such as one from a browser or a JavaScript program, is read as milliseconds. A 10-digit number is read as seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Date to timestamp.</strong> Pick the zone first, then enter the local date and time in that zone. The answer is given in seconds and in milliseconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Daylight saving.</strong> A time that does not exist because clocks jumped forward is read with the offset from before the change. A time that happens twice when clocks go back uses its first occurrence.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <h3>Does this use a live clock or any online data?</h3>
    <p>
      No. Every answer comes from your input and your browser's built-in date and time-zone data. The only thing read
      from your device is today's date when you leave the "age on" box empty.
    </p>
    <h3>Why is my age one day different from another calculator?</h3>
    <p>
      Tools differ on how they treat month ends and 29 February. This page uses the month-end rule described above and
      says so, so you can check the result by hand against the date your form asks for.
    </p>
    <h3>What is the largest date I can enter?</h3>
    <p>
      Dates are accepted from the year 0000 to 9999. A timestamp outside that range shows a message instead of a wrong date.
    </p>
    <h3>Is anything stored or sent?</h3>
    <p>
      No. The values stay in the page while it is open. Read the <a href="/privacy/">privacy page</a> for details.
    </p>
  </div>
  <RelatedTools path="/date-calculators/" />
</Base>
